WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.830 --> 00:00:02.830
- [Narrator] You might've
seen one of these before,

00:00:02.830 --> 00:00:04.533
an at-home DNA test.

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:07.060
The boxes might look small,

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:10.560
but they're making a big
impact on some people's lives.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:13.060
Kits like these have
exploded in popularity

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:14.633
over the past five years.

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:17.370
Most people take them

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:19.710
because they wanna know
more about their background,

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:21.890
like what countries
their ancestors were from

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:24.453
or if they're really related
to George Washington.

00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:28.610
And for many, the results
are what they expect.

00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:29.820
But for others,

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:32.780
the results can radically
change their lives.

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:34.180
- A lotta people take these tests

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:36.230
hoping for something entertaining.

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:38.390
Maybe they heard they're Italian or German

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:41.020
and they think it would
be fun to find out more.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:43.010
But often when they get the results back,

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:45.470
they're surprised by what they find.

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:47.580
- [Narrator] So how can that happen?

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:48.650
Meet Sally.

00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:51.200
We made her up to show how this can work.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.620
Sally took DNA tests with her family,

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:57.370
her mom, her dad, her
brother, and her sister.

00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:00.750
Six weeks later, they went
online to check their results.

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:03.740
The company provided a long
list of genetic matches

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:06.050
ranked in order by the
amount of DNA shared

00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:08.430
and measured in a unit
called centimorgans.

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:10.140
- Often DNA testing companies

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.700
will return a list of your
genetic matches to you.

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:14.500
They usually rank your genetic matches

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:16.110
from the highest number of centimorgans

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:17.180
you share with someone

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:19.860
to down to the fewest
number of centimorgans.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:23.170
So you're gonna expect to
share the most centimorgans

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:25.110
with the people who are closest to you.

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:26.020
- [Narrator] Parents and children

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:27.810
share around half of their DNA.

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:29.650
Siblings share a little bit less.

00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:33.210
Half-siblings share around
a quarter, you get the idea.

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:35.420
So naturally, Sally
saw both of her parents

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:36.800
at the top of the list.

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:40.960
They each shared around 3400
centimorgan in common with her.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:45.240
Next she saw her brother,
who shared 2700 centimorgan.

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:49.490
And then her sister, who she
shared around 1700 centimorgan.

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:52.060
This was the first red flag for Sally.

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:54.023
Why do these numbers look so different?

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:57.970
It turns out 1700 centimorgan

00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:01.690
isn't enough for Sally's
sister to be her full sister.

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:04.970
But it is enough for
them to be half-siblings.

00:02:04.970 --> 00:02:06.350
They talked to their mother

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:09.110
and it turns out she had an affair.

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:10.930
- In our hypothetical example,

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:13.370
Sally found out that her
mother had an affair,

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:15.960
but that's not the only
thing that people find out.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:17.780
Many people have been able to identify

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:20.580
their biological relatives
when they were adopted.

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:21.800
Other people have discovered

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:24.530
that they were conceived
using sperm donations.

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:26.370
- [Narrator] But that wasn't
the only surprise in store

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:28.330
for Sally and her family.

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:31.570
Sally's father Andy checked
out his DNA results too.

00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:33.120
He already knew about the affair,

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:34.290
so he wasn't surprised

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:36.660
that one of his daughters wasn't a match.

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:38.990
But he did notice several
names high up on the list

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:40.850
that he didn't recognize.

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:42.410
When Andy was a college student,

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:45.460
he made sperm donations in
order to earn extra money

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:47.390
and he assumed that his sperm donations

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:49.300
would always remain anonymous.

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:51.840
But now many people who
were donor-conceived

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:54.403
can find their sperm
donors via DNA testing.

00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:58.480
This person shared 3400
centimorgan with Andy,

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:01.163
and as it turned out.
was his biological son.

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:04.460
So Sally's been through a lot.

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:06.980
She's learned that her
sister is her half-sister

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:10.430
and now she has another
half-sibling on her dad's side.

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:12.080
Sally was reeling from the twist

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:14.620
and turns in their family DNA when

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:16.001
the phone rings at Sally's home.

00:03:16.001 --> 00:03:16.834
(phone rings)

00:03:16.834 --> 00:03:18.170
It's the police.

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:20.623
They found Sally's DNA in a database.

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:24.700
- Many people take more than one test.

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:26.600
They want to expand
the number of relatives

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:28.610
they can potentially match with.

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:30.930
Another way to do this
is to take the results

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:34.170
and put them on a website
that has a public database.

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:35.830
Many consumers, if not most,

00:03:35.830 --> 00:03:38.690
are taking these tests
for purposes of genealogy.

00:03:38.690 --> 00:03:40.700
They're not expecting that law enforcement

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:42.610
might be interested in using the results

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:44.990
to track down possible criminals.

00:03:44.990 --> 00:03:46.010
- [Narrator] But increasingly,

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:48.630
law enforcement officials
use the databases as well,

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:51.410
working with genealogists
to try and solve crimes,

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:54.240
mainly cold cases and missing persons.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:56.180
Here's how that works.

00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:58.290
Police take DNA from a crime scene,

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:01.600
it usually comes from the
victim or a suspected criminal.

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:05.120
Then police upload the DNA into
one of the big DNA databases

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:07.910
and look for genetic matches
between the crime scene DNA

00:04:07.910 --> 00:04:10.020
and others who have tested.

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:13.190
- When there is a genetic
match, genealogists are able

00:04:13.190 --> 00:04:15.040
to use the information in the databases

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:17.230
to build a rough family tree.

00:04:17.230 --> 00:04:20.000
They're then able to use
publicly available information,

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:22.560
such as obituaries or
wedding announcements,

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:25.923
which are often available
online, to get more names.

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:30.300
They might look at Facebook and
other social media postings,

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:33.500
again, to find more
connections between people.

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:35.620
Once investigators and genealogists

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:37.520
have created a family tree,

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:39.700
the investigators can
narrow down the leads

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:41.740
and figure out from the family tree

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:43.580
who was born at the most likely time

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:45.030
that the suspect was born,

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:47.160
who was living in the most likely place

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:49.893
where the crime was committed,
or someone disappeared.

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:52.810
- [Narrator] For Sally's family,

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:55.200
the call from the police
helped solve a mystery.

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:57.430
Law enforcement used clues
from their family tree

00:04:57.430 --> 00:04:58.803
to find a missing person.

00:05:00.130 --> 00:05:02.570
To be clear, Sally's experience with DNA

00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:04.350
is not the typical one.

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:07.220
It's not every day that someone
discovers a family affair,

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:10.460
a distant half-brother,
and helps solve a crime.

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:12.440
But all of these things do happen

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:13.730
and they're happening more often

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:16.670
as DNA tests gain popularity.

00:05:16.670 --> 00:05:19.100
- The growing popularity of DNA testing,

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:21.500
and the increasing sizes of the databases,

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:24.070
have really raised privacy concerns.

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:26.440
Decades ago, people
were promised anonymity

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:28.810
when they gave up their
children for adoption

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:31.730
or when they participated
in sperm donation,

00:05:31.730 --> 00:05:34.470
and now suddenly, thanks to DNA testing,

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:35.990
people are able to track down

00:05:35.990 --> 00:05:37.410
the identities of these people.

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:38.450
There are a number of people

00:05:38.450 --> 00:05:40.640
who say that they don't
wanna do DNA testing

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:43.410
because they wanna preserve
their genetic privacy,

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:47.220
but the fact is that even if
you choose not to do a DNA test

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:50.560
a distant cousin that you don't
know and may have never met

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:52.900
is possibly making another decision

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:56.060
and that decision is gonna
affect your privacy as well.

00:05:56.060 --> 00:05:58.170
Some of your DNA may be used by people

00:05:58.170 --> 00:06:01.790
to unravel family secrets or
track down potential criminals

00:06:01.790 --> 00:06:05.050
or all the other uses that
it's currently being used for.

00:06:05.050 --> 00:06:07.853
Even some uses that we
haven't anticipated yet.

